  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes where interface thermal resistance comes from, linking idealized atomic-scale theory to real devices. In the center, two different crystal lattices meet at an interface, with wavy arrows and small radiating marks suggesting heat carriers crossing, reflecting, or scattering. This conveys that resistance arises when vibrational or electronic carriers do not transfer efficiently across the boundary between dissimilar materials.\n\nThe left side groups the main microscopic causes: carrier mismatch between the two solids, interface quality, lattice mismatch with atomic reconstruction, interfacial bonding strength, and surface roughness or disordered layers. Together these factors determine how well energy can pass from one material to the other.\n\nOn the right, simple device cross-sections show that practical packaging and bonded structures add further complications beyond ideal models. These include incomplete physical contact, long-term stability issues, thermo-mechanical failure, and environmental degradation. The layout suggests a progression from fundamental atomic mechanisms to engineering realities, emphasizing that thermal resistance is controlled both by intrinsic material-interface physics and by fabrication quality and reliability in actual devices.",
